<h2>Compare Fractions with the Same Numerator</h2>
<p>Original unit fraction True/False: 1/2&gt;1/4 True;1/4&gt;1/3 False;1/6&gt;1/8 True.</p><h3>Priya and Tyler</h3>
<p>Priya is correct: <strong>5/6 &gt; 5/8</strong>. Both count five unit-sized parts of the SAME whole; eighths are smaller than sixths. Label source five fractions on matching 0–3 number lines: 5/2,5/3,5/4,5/6,5/8. Ordered from smallest to largest: <strong>5/8 &lt;5/6 &lt;5/4 &lt;5/3 &lt;5/2</strong>.</p>
<h3>Original eight fixed comparative statements</h3><table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>First fraction</th><th>Symbol</th><th>Second</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>1/4</td><td>&lt;</td><td>1/3</td></tr><tr><td>3/4</td><td>&gt;</td><td>3/8</td></tr><tr><td>5/3</td><td>&gt;</td><td>5/6</td></tr><tr><td>9/8</td><td>&lt;</td><td>9/6</td></tr><tr><td>2/2</td><td>&gt;</td><td>2/6</td></tr><tr><td>4/3</td><td>&gt;</td><td>4/8</td></tr><tr><td>8/8</td><td>&lt;</td><td>8/4</td></tr><tr><td>5/4</td><td>&lt;</td><td>5/3</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h3>Original four missing denominators (many valid solutions)</h3>
<table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Original prompt</th><th>Condition</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>1/3 &lt; 1/d</td><td>d&lt;3, positive whole d</td></tr><tr><td>6/4 &gt; 6/d</td><td>d&gt;4, positive whole d</td></tr><tr><td>4/4 &lt; 4/d</td><td>d&lt;4, positive whole d</td></tr><tr><td>2/6 &lt; 2/d</td><td>d&lt;6, positive whole d</td></tr></tbody></table>
